Bio: Dawson Roy is a Culture and Technology Studies student at the University of Guelph.
Abstract: This essay examines Novig’s “Just Sports” advertising campaign featuring Sydney Sweeney through the lens of digital storytelling and ethics. It explores how visual choices, celebrity appeal, and the representation of women in sports shape the campaign’s message. The essay also considers responses from female athletes and proposes an alternative approach that highlights their athletic accomplishments.
---

The ad “Just Sports,” created by Novig and featuring actress Sydney Sweeney, introduces a sports prediction market. Sweeney appears in a collection of scenes built around different sports users can trade on the Novig app. She uses sports equipment as props while repeating the campaign’s central message: Novig focuses on sports. Novig’s goal is to sell that identity, distinguishing itself from prediction markets such as Kalshi, Polymarket, and Robinhood, which cover subjects beyond sports. Sweeney is the voice and face of the ad, and her celebrity appeal draws attention through short scenes and a repeated slogan that makes the campaign memorable. As Sweeney explains in Novig’s campaign announcement, “The concept is simple: cut through the noise and put the focus entirely on sports. The campaign brings that idea to life with confidence, humor and a playful edge.”<sup><a href="#note-1">1</a></sup> The tension lies in the ad’s digital storytelling: its use of partial nudity may attract viewers, but it can also unsettle those questioning how women are represented in sports.

The video format lets Novig tell its story through images before viewers have much time to question its message. The dark studio setting removes the usual signs of a sporting event, such as a crowd or competition. Lighting directs attention toward Sweeney, while sports equipment is placed in front of her to cover intimate areas of her body. The quick cuts keep the ad moving, but the partial nudity becomes more noticeable than the sports themselves. This is where the video format shapes the story: viewers hear that Novig is focused on sports but see sports pushed into the background, with a partially nude celebrity taking priority over athletic action.

The opening of the ad asks, “Think you know sports?”<sup><a href="#note-2">2</a></sup> However, the video places more emphasis on Sweeney’s appearance than on women’s athletic knowledge or achievements. Female athletes have responded to the campaign, questioning the choices made by Novig and Sweeney. Olympic swimmer Ariarne Titmus described the ad as a “kick in the face” to female athletes, while Amy Hunt and Sophie Capewell also criticized the campaign. Reactions were not uniform: WNBA player Sophie Cunningham expressed discomfort with the ad while acknowledging Sweeney’s freedom to participate.<sup><a href="#note-3">3</a></sup> This connects to Hill’s discussion of the politics of storytelling and the ways an organization’s goals shape the story it tells.<sup><a href="#note-4">4</a></sup> Novig’s choice to highlight a celebrity helps gain attention but also shapes which perspective on sports viewers see. Cunningham’s response shows that it is possible to respect Sweeney’s decision while still questioning Novig’s decision to leave female athletic accomplishments out of the ad.

Novig could have kept Sweeney in the ad while giving female athletes a visible role. Showing them training or competing alongside her would have supported the claim that the campaign focuses on sports. Sweeney’s presence would still attract public attention, but the video would give viewers athletic stories to remember too.

